<text x="12.7" y="701.04" size="2.54" layer="97" rot="MR180">Click Modules come with I2C pull ups on-board.  
Footprints added on I2C1 and I2C3 for convenience (see other page).</text>
<text x="99.06" y="711.2" size="3.81" layer="150" rot="R180" align="top-left">PA</text>
<text x="175.26" y="711.2" size="3.81" layer="150" rot="R180" align="top-left">PB</text>
<text x="271.78" y="711.2" size="3.81" layer="150" rot="R180" align="top-left">PC</text>
<text x="347.98" y="711.2" size="3.81" layer="150" rot="R180" align="top-left">PD</text>
<text x="655.32" y="342.9" size="2.54" layer="97" rot="MR180">Click 0</text>
<text x="655.32" y="335.28" size="2.54" layer="97" rot="MR180">Click 1</text>
<text x="1193.8" y="322.58" size="2.54" layer="97" rot="MR180">DFP Advertisement:  
    Default USB Power (500mA/900mA)</text>
<text x="406.4" y="538.48" size="2.54" layer="97" rot="MR180">UFP Request:  3.0A</text>
<text x="403.86" y="106.68" size="3.81" layer="97" align="top-left">USB-C Power Discussion</text>
<text x="403.86" y="99.06" size="2.54" layer="97" align="top-left">The USB-C UFP uses 5.1k pull down resistors on the CC pins to request up to 3A from the DFP.  However, this board does not check the voltage of the CC pins to understand the current provided by the DFP port.  The USB-C DFP port 
on this board uses 56k ohm pull up resistors to advertise 500mA of current availlable.  It is the resposibility of the user to guarantee that there is enough current to support both the components on the board as welll as any downstream
USB devices.</text>
