<text x="1054.1" y="81.28" size="6.4516" layer="97">VPP LDO</text>
<wire x1="1165.86" y1="93.98" x2="1051.56" y2="93.98" width="0.762" layer="97"/>
<wire x1="1046.48" y1="88.9" x2="1046.48" y2="30.48" width="0.762" layer="97"/>
<wire x1="1432.56" y1="83.82" x2="1432.56" y2="254" width="0.762" layer="97"/>
<text x="1440.18" y="236.22" size="2.54" layer="97" rot="MR180">These pins are reserved and should not be connected or routed thru.</text>
<text x="797.56" y="238.76" size="2.54" layer="97" rot="MR180">Power Connections for the OSD62-PM</text>
<text x="1054.1" y="78.74" size="2.54" layer="97" rot="MR180">To program internal OTP memory (security keys, etc.), VPP must be 
supplied by an externally controllable power supply.  

Jumper J1 must be installed to program the OTP memory.</text>
<text x="137.16" y="345.44" size="2.54" layer="97" align="top-left">Note:  MCU_ERRORn must be connected to VSS with external pull resistor 
in order to hold the pin to a logic LOW level if connected to another device 
or if it has a long trace to a test point that could be suseptible to noise.
</text>
<text x="1506.22" y="487.68" size="2.54" layer="97" align="top-left">Note:  It is recommended to add a 
pull-down on MMC_CLK signal to 
hold the signal LOW when AM62x 
IO buffers are not active.
</text>
<text x="965.2" y="370.84" size="1.778" layer="150" align="top-left">GPO2 is enabled in the TPS6521903 configuration 
and used by the PMIC to enable the 3.3V load switch.</text>
<text x="825.5" y="360.68" size="1.778" layer="150" align="top-left">See https://www.ti.com/lit/ug/slvucj2a/slvucj2a.pdf for more information on the "03" configuration of the TPS65219. </text>
<text x="825.5" y="355.6" size="1.778" layer="150" align="top-left">See https://octavosystems.com/app_notes/osd62x-pm-power-application-note/ for an in-depth discusion of the TPS65219. </text>
</plain>
